+++
!!Tyre!![[ ^1 ]] is a city in [[ Lebanon ]], and one of the
[[ oldest continuously inhabited cities | list-of-oldest-continuously-inhabited-cities ]]
in the world.[[ ^1 ]] It was one of the earliest [[ Phoenician | phoenicia ]]
[[ metropolises | metropolis ]] and the legendary birthplace of
[[ Europa | europa-consort-of-zeus ]], her brothers [[ Cadmus ]] and
[[ Phoenix | phoenix-son-of-agenor ]], and [[ Carthage ]]'s founder [[ Dido ]]
(Elissa). The city has many ancient sites, including the
[[ Tyre Hippodrome ]], and was added as a whole to the list of [[ UNESCO ]]
[[ World Heritage Sites | world-heritage-site ]] in 1984.[[ ^2 ]] The
historian [[ Ernest Renan ]] described it as "a city of ruins, built out of
ruins".[[ ^3 ]][[ ^4 ]]

Tyre is the fifth-largest city in Lebanon after [[ Beirut ]],
[[ Tripoli | tripoli-lebanon ]], [[ Sidon ]], and [[ Baalbek ]].[[ ^5 ]] It is
the capital of the [[ Tyre District ]] in the [[ South Governorate ]]. There
were approximately 200,000 inhabitants in the Tyre urban area in 2016,
including many [[ refugees | refugee ]], as the city hosts three of the twelve
[[ Palestinian refugee camps ]] in Lebanon:
[[ Burj El Shimali | burj-el-shamali ]], [[ El Buss | el-buss-refugee-camp ]],
and [[ Rashidieh ]].[[ ^6 ]]

== Territory

Tyre juts out from the coast of the [[ Mediterranean Sea ]], and is located
about 80 km (50 mi) south of [[ Beirut ]]. It originally consisted of two
distinct urban centres: Tyre itself, which was on an island just 500 to 700
metres (yards) offshore; and the associated settlement of [[ Ushu ]] on the
adjacent mainland, later called //Palaetyrus// (Greek: Παλαίτυρος), meaning
"Old Tyre" in [[ Ancient Greek ]].[[ ^7 ]] The fortified city was on top of a
rock from which its name was inherited, as //S'r// is the Phoenician word for
"rock". It had two ports, the "Sidonian port" to the north, still partly
existing today, and the "Egyptian port" to the south which very recently has
perhaps been discovered.[[ ^8 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/OffTyreRockyIslands_RomanDeckert04102019.jpg/250px-OffTyreRockyIslands_RomanDeckert04102019.jpg" height="187" width="250"

  <figcaption>Rocky islets off Tyre

From [[ prehistoric times | prehistory ]] onwards, all settlements in the Tyre
area profited from the abundance of [[ fresh water ]] supplies, especially
from the nearby [[ springs | spring-hydrology ]] of Rashidieh and
[[ Ras Al Ain | ras-al-ain-lebanon ]] in the South. In addition, there are the
springs of Al Bagbog and Ain Ebreen in the North as well as the
[[ Litani River ]], also known as Alqasymieh.[[ ^9 ]] The present city of Tyre
covers a large part of the original island and has expanded onto and covers
most of the [[ causeway ]] built by [[ Alexander the Great ]] in 332
[[ BCE | common-era ]].[[ ^10 ]] This [[ isthmus ]] increased greatly in width
over the centuries because of extensive silt depositions on either side. The
part of the original island not covered by the modern city of Tyre is mostly
of an archaeological site showcasing remains of the city from ancient times.

Four municipalities contribute to Tyre city's 16.7-square-kilometre
(6+7⁄16 sq mi) built-up area, though none are included in their entirety: Sour
municipality contains the heart of the city, excluding the Natural and Coastal
Reserve; Burj El Shimali to the East without unpopulated agricultural lands;
Abbasiyet Sour to the North without agricultural lands and a dislocated
village; and [[ Ain Baal ]] to the South-East, also without agricultural lands
and dislocated villages. Tyre's urban area lies on a fertile coastal plain,
which explains the fact that as of 2017 about 44% of its territory was used
for intra-urban agriculture, while built-up land constituted over 40%.[[ ^6 ]]

In terms of [[ geomorphology ]] and [[ seismicity ]], Tyre is close to the
Roum Fault and the [[ Yammouneh | yammoune ]] [[ Fault | fault-geology ]].
Though it has suffered a number of devastating earthquakes over the
[[ millennia | millennium ]], the threat level is considered to be low in most
places and moderate in a few others. However, a [[ tsunami ]] following an
earthquake and subsequent [[ landslides | landslide ]] and floods pose major
natural risks to the Tyrian population.[[ ^6 ]]

Vast reserves of natural gas are estimated to lie beneath Lebanese waters,
much of it off Tyre's coast, but
[[ exploitation | exploitation-of-natural-resources ]] has been delayed by
[[ border disputes | territorial-dispute ]] with Israel.[[ ^11 ]]

== Etymology

Early names of Tyre include [[ Akkadian | akkadian-language ]] //Ṣurru//,
[[ Phoenician | phoenician-languages ]] //Ṣūr// (𐤑𐤓‎), and
[[ Hebrew | hebrew-language ]] //Ṣōr// (צוֹר).[[ ^12 ]] In
[[ Semitic languages ]], the name of the city possibly means 'rock'[[ ^13 ]]
after the rocky formation on which the town was originally built.

The predominant form in [[ Classical Greek ]] was //Týros// (Τύρος), which was
first seen in the works of [[ Herodotus ]] but may have been adopted
considerably earlier.[[ ^12 ]] It gave rise to [[ Latin ]] //Tyrus//, which
entered [[ English | english-language ]] during the [[ Middle English ]]
period as //Tyre//.[[ ^14 ]] The [[ demonym ]] for Tyre is //Tyrian//, and the
inhabitants are //Tyrians//.[[ ^15 ]]

== Climate

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/TyreAlMinaCitySite_RoundRainbow_RomanDeckert11122020.jpg/250px-TyreAlMinaCitySite_RoundRainbow_RomanDeckert11122020.jpg" alt="" height="186" width="250"

  <figcaption>A [[ 22° halo ]] over Al Mina site, 2019

Tyre has a [[ Hot-summer mediterranean climate | mediterranean-climate ]]
(classified as //Csa// under the [[ Köppen climate classification ]]),
characterized by six months of drought from May to October. On average, it has
300 days of [[ sun ]] a year and a yearly temperature of
20.8[[ °C | celsius ]] (69.5 °F). The average maximum temperature reaches its
highest at 30.8 °C (87.5 °F) in August and the average minimum temperature its
lowest at 10 °C (50 °F) in January. On average, the mean annual
[[ precipitation ]] reaches up to 645 mm (25½"). The temperature of the
[[ sea water | seawater ]] reaches a minimum of 17 °C (63°F) in February and a
maximum of 32 °C (90°F) in August. At a depth of 70 metres (200') it is
constantly at 17–18 °C (63 to 64°F).[[ ^16 ]]

Meanwhile, [[ rising sea levels | sea-level-rise ]] due to
[[ global warming ]] threaten [[ coastal erosion ]] to Tyre's peninsula and
bay areas.[[ ^17 ]]

