== History

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Album_de_voyage_en_Orient%2C_CSR_AG_318%2804%29.jpg/250px-Album_de_voyage_en_Orient%2C_CSR_AG_318%2804%29.jpg" height="173" width="250"

  <figcaption>[[ Ary Renan ]]'s 1878 sketch of Tyre

The ancient city of Tyre is located along the coast of [[ Phoenicia ]] in
modern Lebanon. The site has been occupied since the
[[ Bronze Age ]].[[ ^19 ]] The city became a prominent Phoenician city-state
between the 9th and 6th centuries BC, settling prestigious colonies around the
Mediterranean Sea, such as [[ Carthage ]] and [[ Leptis Magna ]].[[ ^20 ]] It
came under Babylonian rule in 572 BC, before being conquered by [[ siege ]] by
[[ Alexander the Great ]] in 332 BC. Eight thousand Tyrians died in the
massacre that followed, against only 400 Macedonian deaths; 30,000 surviving
Tyrians were sold into [[ slavery ]].[[ ^21 ]]

Monumental archaeological remains dated from the subsequent
[[ Hellenistic | hellenistic-period ]], [[ Roman | roman-empire ]],
[[ Byzantine | byzantine-empire ]], and [[ Medieval | middle-ages ]] periods
led to its inscription on its archaeological remains on the [[ UNESCO ]]
World's Heritage list in 1984.

The Roman historian [[ Justin | justin-historian ]] wrote that the original
founders arrived from the nearby city of [[ Sidon ]] in the quest to establish
a new harbour. The Greek historian [[ Herodotus ]] (c. 484–425 BC) visited
Tyre around 450 BC at the end of the [[ Greco-Persian Wars ]] (499–449 BC),
and wrote in his //[[ Histories | histories-herodotus ]]// that according to
the priests there, the city was founded 2300 years earlier (around 2750
BC),[[ ^22 ]] as a walled place upon the mainland, now known as //Paleotyre//
(Old Tyre).

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/A_pictorial_commentary_on_the_Gospel_according_to_Mark_-_with_the_text_of_the_Authorized_and_Revised_versions_%281881%29_%2814780353464%29.jpg/250px-A_pictorial_commentary_on_the_Gospel_according_to_Mark_-_with_the_text_of_the_Authorized_and_Revised_versions_%281881%29_%2814780353464%29.jpg" height="164" width="250"

  <figcaption>1881 sketch of the skyline of Tyre

The Phoenician Tyrians' international trade network was based on its two
harbours which are mentioned by ancient writers.[[ ^23 ]][[ ^24 ]] The
northern harbour opened toward the Phoenician city of [[ Sidon ]] and has been
therefore referred to as the "Sidonian Harbour" by 19th and 20th century
scholars, but it was referred to as the "Port of Astronoe" during Late
Antiquity.[[ ^25 ]] The southern harbour opened toward [[ Egypt ]] and was
referred to as the "Egyptian Harbour". The location of the two harbours has
been the subject of speculations since the 17th Century.[[ ^26 ]] The
submarine excavation of a large, 4-6th Century BC breakwater north of the
city,[[ ^27 ]][[ ^28 ]] and the discovery of 250 BC to 500 AD harbour
sediments behind this breakwater[[ ^29 ]] demonstrated the existence of a
northern harbour repeatedly, if not permanently, throughout Antiquity under
the modern harbour of Tyre.

The location of the southern harbour is more elusive. Renan envisioned it as
an extensive structure now located offshore, south of the former island.
Subsequent diving surveys identified submerged man-made structures on the
seafloor within 150 m of the former island.[[ ^30 ]][[ ^31 ]] Antoine
Poidebard, who was the first to have them explored by divers in 1939, saw in
these structures former breakwaters enclosing a harbour with two
entrances.[[ ^32 ]] The geographic area enclosed within these structures is
therefore often referred to as the "Southern Harbour". These structures have
also been interpreted as a polder-like area protecting an urban
district.[[ ^33 ]] A Phoenician-style breakwater was recently found within
this area, but excavation is needed to confirm its age ascription.[[ ^34 ]]
Harbour sediments found behind the structure suggest that the breakwater was
part of the Egyptian harbour.[[ ^35 ]][[ ^36 ]] Harbour sediments found near
Hiram's Tower, further north, mark an early location of the Sidonian
Harbour.[[ ^35 ]]

The development of Tyre was profoundly affected by the construction of a
[[ causeway ]] built by [[ Alexander the Great ]] in 332 BC to seize the
city.[[ ^10 ]] This reportedly 750 metre (820 yard) long[[ ^37 ]] and 60 metre
(200 ft.) wide causeway was laid over a submarine [[ shoal ]] less than 5.4
metres (18 ft.) deep.[[ ^38 ]] This shoal was interpreted as a sandbank (also
called a 'salient'), formed by the accretion of sand in the lee of the island,
under the effects of the refraction and diffraction of waves around the
island. The causeway interrupted longshore sand transport, forcing sand to
accumulate along the causeway, rapidly creating an emerged sandy isthmus (or
[[ tombolo ]]), linking the island to the mainland.[[ ^39 ]][[ ^40 ]]

This sandy isthmus rapidly inflated during the centuries following the
construction of the causeway. By early Imperial Roman times, monumental
buildings had been built over most of its surface. Their layout implies that
the isthmus was by then nearly as wide as today. Therefore, the isthmus had
completely reshaped the eastern coast of Tyre Island within 6–10 centuries
after the construction of the causeway, spurring a radical transformation of
the city.

== Coast Nature Reserve

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/SeaTurleTyre-RomanDeckert11092019.jpg/250px-SeaTurleTyre-RomanDeckert11092019.jpg" height="163" width="218"

A sea turtle off Tyre's Southern bay

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/GreenTurtle_TyreSourLebanon_SubmergedAncientHarbour_RomanDeckert18102019.jpg/250px-GreenTurtle_TyreSourLebanon_SubmergedAncientHarbour_RomanDeckert18102019.jpg" height="164" width="218"

A [[ green sea turtle ]] diving through submerged antiquities (Quay of the
Spring)

Tyre enjoys a reputation of having some of the cleanest beaches and waters of
Lebanon.[[ ^41 ]][[ ^42 ]] However, a UN HABITAT profile found that "seawater
is also polluted due to wastewater discharge especially in the port
area".[[ ^6 ]] There is still also considerable pollution by solid
waste.[[ ^43 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/PalestineSunbird_TyreSourLebanon_RomanDeckert03122019.jpg/250px-PalestineSunbird_TyreSourLebanon_RomanDeckert03122019.jpg" height="241" width="250"

  <figcaption>A [[ Palestine sunbird ]] on //[[ Tecoma capensis ]]// near Al Mina

The [[ Tyre Coast Nature Reserve ]] (TCNR) was decreed in 1998 by the
[[ Ministry of Public Works | ministry-of-public-works-and-transport-lebanon ]].
It is 3.5 km (2.2 mi) long and covers over 380 hectares (940 acres). The TCNR
is within the best preserved stretch of sandy coastline in southern Lebanon
and divided into two section zones: a 1.8 km (1 mile) sand lined beach, 500
meters (yards) wide-ranging from the Tyre Rest House in the north to the
Rashidieh Refugee Camp in the South, and a stretch of 2 km (1¼ miles) with
agriculture lands of small family farms and the springs of Ras El Ain with
three constantly flowing [[ artesian wells | artesian-aquifer ]], ranging from
Rashidieh to the village of Chaetiyeh in the South.[[ ^16 ]]

The former is divided into two zones: one for tourism that features a public
beach of some 900 metres (yards) and restaurant tents during the summer season
hosting up to 20,000 visitors on a busy day, and another 900 metres (yards) of
conservation zone as a sanctuary for sea turtles and migrating birds.[[ ^43 ]]

Due to its diverse [[ flora ]] and [[ fauna ]], the reserve was designated a
[[ Ramsar Site | ramsar-convention ]] in 1999 according to the international
[[ treaty ]] for the conservation and sustainable use of
[[ Wetlands | wetland ]], since it is considered "the last
[[ bio-geographic | biogeography ]] [[ ecosystem ]] in Lebanon". It is an
important nesting site for [[ migratory birds | bird-migration ]], the
endangered [[ Loggerhead | loggerhead-sea-turtle ]] and
[[ green sea turtle ]], the [[ Arabian spiny mouse ]] and many other creatures
(including [[ wall lizards | lacertidae ]], [[ common pipistrelle ]], and
[[ European badger ]]).[[ ^44 ]][[ ^45 ]] Also, there are frequent sighting of
dolphins in the waters off Tyre.[[ ^46 ]] Altogether, the TCNR includes:

<blockquote
  275 [[ species ]] distributed over 50 families. In addition, the reserve is
  home to seven regionally and nationally [[ threatened species ]], 4
  [[ endemic | endemism ]] and 10 [[ rare species ]], whilst 59 species are
  restricted to the Eastern Mediterranean area. It is also worthy to indicate
  that, several [[ bio-indicator | bioindicator ]] species as well as 25
  [[ medicinal | medicinal-plants ]] species were recognized. TCNR encloses
  flora species belonging to the various habitats: the sandy shore, rocky shore,
  [[ littoral | littoral-zone ]] and
  [[ Freshwater ecosystems | freshwater-ecosystem ]]. A wide number of
  [[ Gramineae | poaceae ]], [[ Fabaceae ]], [[ Asteraceae ]] and
  [[ Umbellifereae | apiaceae ]] families dominate the floristic
  resources.[[ ^16 ]]

However, the biodiversity of the TCNR is threatened as shown by a strong
decrease in the numbers of the caspian [[ terrapin ]]
//[[ Mauremys caspica | caspian-turtle ]]//, the green [[ toad ]]
//[[ Bufo viridis | european-green-toad ]]// and the [[ tree frog ]]
//[[ Hyla savigny | hyla-savignyi ]]//. Also, since the 2000s, the North
American camphorweed //[[ Heterotheca subaxillaris ]]// has invaded the TCNR
as a [[ neophyte | neophyte-botany ]] from Haifa across the Blue
Line.[[ ^16 ]]

During the 2006 war, turtle breeding areas were affected when the IDF bombed
the conservation site.[[ ^47 ]]

The [[ oil spill ]] which devastated the coast north of [[ Ashkelon ]] in
February 2021 also contaminated Tyre's beaches.[[ ^48 ]]

== Historical and cultural heritage

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Tyre_in_Lebanon_marking_as_protected_cultural_property.jpg/250px-Tyre_in_Lebanon_marking_as_protected_cultural_property.jpg" height="167" width="250"

  <figcaption>Sign marking Tyre according to the 1954 [[ Hague Convention for the Protection of Cultural Property in the Event of Armed Conflict ]]. Note the ruins of the Mamluk House (left) which has been rehabilitated since.

Arguably the most lasting Phoenician legacy for the Tyrian population has been
the linguistic mark that the [[ Syriac | syriac-language ]] and
[[ Akkadian | akkadian-language ]] languages have left on the Arabic spoken in
the region of Tyre.[[ ^49 ]] Most notably, the widely used term "Ba'ali" –
which is used especially to describe vegetables and fruits from rain-fed,
untreated agricultural production – originates from the Baal
religion.[[ ^50 ]] The Tyrian municipality of [[ Ain Baal ]] is apparently
also named after the Phoenician deity.[[ ^51 ]] The most visible part of
ancient and medieval history on the other side have been the archaeological
sites though:

The first archaeological excavations were by Ernest Renan in 1860 and
1861.[[ ^26 ]] He was followed in the 1870s by [[ Johann Nepomuk Sepp ]]. His
most notable work was excavating at the
[[ Cathedral of the Holy Cross | cathedral-of-the-holy-cross-tyre ]] in an
attempt to find the bones of [[ Frederick Barbarossa ]].[[ ^52 ]] More work
was undertaken in 1903 by the Ottoman archaeologist [[ Theodore Makridi ]],
curator of the Imperial Museum at [[ Constantinople ]]. Important findings
like fragments of marble sarcophagi were sent to the Ottoman
capital.[[ ^53 ]][[ ^54 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/TyreAlMinaCitySite_MauriceChehabExcavationsRemnants-Rail-Wheelbarrow_RomanDeckert23082019.jpg/250px-TyreAlMinaCitySite_MauriceChehabExcavationsRemnants-Rail-Wheelbarrow_RomanDeckert23082019.jpg" height="186" width="250"

  <figcaption>Rusty relics from Chehab's excavations at Al Mina site

An archaeological survey of Tyre was done by a French team under the
leadership of Denyse Le Lasseur in 1921.[[ ^55 ]] It was followed by another
mission between 1934 and 1936 that included aerial surveys and diving
expeditions. It was led by the [[ Jesuit ]] missionary
[[ Antoine Poidebard ]], a pioneer of [[ aerial archaeology ]].[[ ^56 ]]

Large-scale excavations started in 1946 under the leadership of [[ Emir ]]
[[ Maurice Chéhab | maurice-chehab ]] (1904–1994), "the father of modern
Lebanese archaeology" who for decades headed the Antiquities Service in
Lebanon and was the curator of the [[ National Museum of Beirut ]]. His teams
uncovered most remains in the
[[ Al Bass | tyre-necropolis ]]/[[ Hippodrome | tyre-hippodrome ]] and the
City Site/Roman baths.[[ ^57 ]][[ ^58 ]][[ ^59 ]]

During the 1960s, [[ Honor Frost ]] (1917–2010) – the [[ Cyprus ]]-born
pioneer of [[ underwater archaeology ]] initiated several investigations
"aimed at identifying and documenting the significant archaeological potential
for harbour facilities within coastal Tyre". Based on the results, she
suggested that the Al Mobarakee Tower may actually date back to Hellenistic
times.[[ ^60 ]]

All those works stopped though soon after the 1975 beginning of the Civil War
and many records were lost.[[ ^57 ]]

In 1984, the
[[ United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organisation (UNESCO) | unesco ]]
declared Tyre a [[ World Heritage Site ]] in an attempt to halt the damage
being done to the archaeological sites by the armed conflict and by anarchic
urban development.[[ ^41 ]]

In the late 1980s, "clandestine excavations" took place in the
[[ Al-Bass cemetery | tyre-necropolis ]], which "flooded the antiquities
market".[[ ^61 ]]

Regular excavation activities only started again in 1995 under the supervision
of Ali Khalil Badawi.[[ ^62 ]] Shortly afterwards, an Israeli bomb destroyed
an apartment block in the city and evidence for an early church was revealed
underneath the rubble. Its unusual design suggests that this was the site of
the Cathedral of Paulinus which had been inaugurated in 315 CE.[[ ^63 ]]

In 1997, the first Phoenician cremation cemetery was uncovered in the
[[ al-Bass site | tyre-necropolis ]], near the Roman necropolis.[[ ^64 ]]
Meanwhile, Honor Frost mentored local Lebanese archaeologists to conduct
further underwater investigations, which in 2001 confirmed the existence of a
human-made structure within the northern harbour area of Tyre.[[ ^60 ]]

In 2003, Randa Berri, president of the National Association for the
Preservation of South Lebanon's Archaeology and Heritage and wife of
[[ Nabih Berri ]], veteran leader of the [[ Amal Movement ]] and longtime
Speaker of the Parliament of Lebanon, patronized a plan to renovate Khan Sour
/ Khan Al Askaar, the former Ma'ani palace, and convert it into a
museum.[[ ^65 ]] As of 2019, nothing was done in that regard and the ruins
have kept on crumbling.

The hostilities of the [[ 2006 Lebanon War ]] put the ancient structures of
Tyre at risk. This prompted [[ UNESCO ]]'s Director-General to launch a
"Heritage Alert" for the site.[[ ^66 ]] Following the cessation of hostilities
in September 2006, a visit by conservation experts to Lebanon observed no
direct damage to the ancient city of Tyre. However, the bombardment had
damaged frescoes in a Roman funerary cave at the [[ Tyre Necropolis ]].
Additional site degradation was also noted, including "the lack of
maintenance, the decay of exposed structures due to lack of rainwater
regulation and the decay of porous and soft stones".[[ ^67 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/TyreCitySite_ArchaeologicalExcavations_UniversityOfLyon_RomanDeckert09102019.jpg/250px-TyreCitySite_ArchaeologicalExcavations_UniversityOfLyon_RomanDeckert09102019.jpg" height="188" width="250"

  <figcaption>Archaeologists from the University of Lyon at the Al Mina/City site in 2019

Since 2008, a [[ Lebanese/French | lebanese-french ]] team under the direction
by Pierre-Louis Gatier of the [[ University of Lyon ]] has been conducting
archaeological and topographical work. When international archeological
missions in [[ Syria ]] came to a halt after 2012 due to the war there, some
of them instead started excavations in Tyre, amongst them a team headed by
[[ Leila Badre ]], director of the
[[ Archeological Museum of the American University of Beirut | archaeological-museum-of-the-american-university-of-beirut ]]
([[ AUB | american-university-of-beirut ]]), and Belgian
archaeologists.[[ ^57 ]]

Threats to Tyre's ancient [[ cultural heritage ]] include development
pressures and the illegal antiquities trade.[[ ^68 ]] A highway, planned for
2011, was expected to be built in areas that are deemed archaeologically
sensitive.[[ ^69 ]] A small-scale geophysical survey indicated the presence of
archaeological remains at proposed construction sites. The sites have not been
investigated. Despite the relocation of a proposed traffic interchange, the
lack of precise site boundaries confuses the issue of site
preservation.[[ ^67 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Tyre_KhanRabu-ruins_MainroomBalcony_RomanDeckert21112019.jpg/250px-Tyre_KhanRabu-ruins_MainroomBalcony_RomanDeckert21112019.jpg" height="187" width="250"

  <figcaption>The ruins of Khan Rabu

A 2018 study of Mediterranean world heritage sites found that Tyre's City site
has "the highest risk of coastal erosion under current climatic conditions, in
addition to 'moderate' risk from extreme sea levels."[[ ^70 ]] Further coastal
inspection was conducted in 2019, leading to a new hypothesis about the local
relative sea level rise and to discovery of yet unreported submerged coastal
structures.[[ ^71 ]]

Like many of the cities in the Levant and in [[ Lebanon ]], the architecture
since the [[ Lebanese Civil War ]] in the 1970s has been of poor quality,
which tend to threaten the cultural heritage in the built environment before
the war.[[ ^72 ]][[ ^73 ]] Meanwhile, historical buildings from the Ottoman
period like Khan Rabu and Khan Sour / Khan Ashkar have partly collapsed after
decades of total neglect and lack of any maintenance whatsoever.[[ ^73 ]]

In 2013, the International Association to Save Tyre (IAST) made headlines when
it launched an online [[ raffle ]] in association with [[ Sotheby's ]] to fund
the artisans' village Les Ateliers de Tyr at the outskirts of the city.
Participants could purchase tickets for 100 euros to win the 1914 //Man in the
Opera Hat// painting by [[ Pablo Picasso ]].[[ ^74 ]] The proceeds totaled
US$5.26 million. The painting was won by a 25-year-old fire-safety official
from [[ Pennsylvania ]].[[ ^75 ]] IAST president [[ Maha al-Khalil Chalabi ]]
is a daughter of feudal lord and politician
[[ Kazem el-Khalil | kazem-al-khalil ]].[[ ^76 ]] In September 2017, she
opened "Les Atelier", which is located in the middle of an orange grove
covering an area of 7.300 m^^2^^ at the northeastern outskirts of
Tyre.[[ ^77 ]]

During the [[ 2024 Israeli invasion of Lebanon ]], [[ UNESCO ]] gave enhanced
protection to 34 cultural sites in Lebanon including the Tyre archaeological
sites to safeguard them from
[[ damage | destruction-of-cultural-heritage-during-the-israeli-invasion-of-lebanon ]].[[ ^78 ]][[ ^79 ]]

=== Biblical description

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/John_Martin_-_Destruction_of_Tyre_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/250px-John_Martin_-_Destruction_of_Tyre_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" height="191" width="250"

  <figcaption>The prophesied destruction of Tyre as painted by [[ John Martin | john-martin-painter ]].

The city of Tyre appears in many biblical traditions:

==== Hebrew Bible / Old Testament

- According to the [[ Hebrew Bible’s | hebrew-bible ]][[ Book of Joshua ]] 19, "the fortified city of Tyre" was allotted to the [[ Israelite | israelites ]][[ Tribe of Asher ]].[[ ^80 ]]
- [[ King Hiram I ]] of Tyre allied himself with [[ King David ]] and [[ King Solomon ]] in [[ 2 Samuel | books-of-samuel ]], [[ 1 Kings | books-of-samuel ]] and [[ 1 Chronicles | books-of-chronicles ]].[[ ^81 ]] Hiram provided architects, workmen, [[ cedar wood | cedar-of-lebanon ]], and gold to build the royal palace in [[ Jerusalem ]], as well as the [[ Temple | solomons-temple ]].[[ ^82 ]]
- Tyre is listed among an alliance of ten nations that would conspire against God's people.[[ ^83 ]]
- Tyre is mentioned in the [[ Book of Isaiah ]] as being forgotten for 70 years when her "fortress is destroyed" and after which "her profit and her prostitute's wages will be sacred to the Lord."[[ ^84 ]]
- The [[ Book of Joel ]] groups Tyre, [[ Sidon ]] and [[ Philistia ]] together and it states that the people of [[ Judah | judah-son-of-jacob ]] and Jerusalem were sold to the [[ Greeks | ancient-greece ]], and there would thus be punishment because of it.[[ ^85 ]]
- Tyre is also mentioned in the [[ Book of Ezekiel ]],[[ ^86 ]][[ Book of Amos ]],[[ ^87 ]] the [[ Psalms ]], and the [[ Book of Zechariah ]][[ ^88 ]] which [[ prophesied | prophecy ]] its destruction.

==== New Testament

- [[ Jesus ]] visited the region or "coasts" ([[ King James Version ]]) of Tyre and [[ Sidon ]][[ ^89 ]] and from this region many came forth to hear him preaching,[[ ^90 ]] leading to the stark contrast in [[ Matthew | gospel-of-matthew ]] 11:21[[ ^91 ]] to his reception in [[ Korazin ]] and [[ Bethsaida ]].
- [[ Herod Antipas ]] was said to be angry with the people of Tyre and Sidon and he delivered a public address upon which he was struck down by God after not giving glory to him once he received praise arrogantly according to the [[ Book of Acts | acts-of-the-apostles ]].[[ ^92 ]] The same book describes Paul's voyage to Tyre where he stayed for seven days.[[ ^93 ]]
- In the [[ Book of Revelation ]],[[ ^94 ]] chapter 18 alludes extensively to the mercantile description of Tyre in Ezekiel 26–28.

=== Other writings

- //[[ Apollonius of Tyre ]]// is the subject of an ancient short novella, popular in the Middle Ages. Existing in numerous forms in many languages, the text is thought to be translated from an ancient Greek manuscript, now lost.
- //[[ Pericles, Prince of Tyre ]]// is a [[ Jacobean | jacobean-era ]] play written at least in part by [[ William Shakespeare ]] and [[ George Wilkins ]]. It is included in modern editions of his collected works despite questions over its authorship.
- In 19th-century Britain, Tyre was several times taken as an [[ exemplar | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/exemplar ]] of the mortality of great power and status, for example by [[ John Ruskin ]] in the opening lines of //[[ The Stones of Venice | the-stones-of-venice-book ]]// and by [[ Rudyard Kipling ]]'s [[ Recessional | recessional-poem ]].
- Tyrus is the title and subject of a poem by the [[ Cumbrian | lake-district ]] poet [[ Norman Nicholson ]] in his collection //Rock Face// of 1948.
- The French [[ comic book artist ]][[ Albert Uderzo ]] published in 1981 //[[ Asterix and the Black Gold ]]// which describes [[ Asterix ]]'s and [[ Obelix ]]'s voyage to the Middle East featuring [[ James Bond ]] and biblical themes: in their quest for petroleum, they sail on board a Phoenician ship, but the Roman regime closes off the ports of Tyre in order to deny their landing.
- In 2015, the [[ French Lebanese | french-people-in-lebanon ]] artist Joseph Safieddine published the [[ graphic novel ]] drama //Yallah Bye// which offers an account of his family's fate during the 2006 war between Israel and [[ Hezbollah ]], when they sought refuge in the Christian quarter of Tyre. An English version followed in 2017 and an Arabic one in 2019.

=== Astronomical objects

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/PIA01633_Tyre_impact_structure_Europa.jpg/250px-PIA01633_Tyre_impact_structure_Europa.jpg" height="146" width="162"

Tyre on Europa

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/209_Dido.png/330px-209_Dido.png" height="146" width="262"

The orbit of 209 Dido

A multi-ring structured region on [[ Europa | europa-moon ]], the smallest of
the four [[ Galilean moons ]] orbiting [[ Jupiter ]], is named after Tyre, the
legendary birthplace of princess Europa. Originally called "Tyre
[[ Macula | macula-planetary-geology ]]", it is some 140 kilometers (90 miles)
in [[ diameter ]] (about the size of the
[[ island of Hawaii | hawaii-island ]]) and thought to be the site where an
[[ asteroid ]] or [[ comet ]] impacted Europa's ice crust.[[ ^95 ]]

The asteroid [[ 209 Dido ]] is named after the legendary Tyrian-Carthaginian
princess. It is a very large [[ main-belt | asteroid-belt ]] [[ asteroid ]],
classified as a [[ C-type asteroid ]] which is probably composed of
[[ carbonaceous | carbonate ]] materials. 209 Dido was discovered in 1879 by
[[ C. H. F. Peters | christian-heinrich-friedrich-peters ]].

== Cultural life

The first cinema in Tyre opened in the late 1930s when a café owner
established makeshift film screenings.[[ ^96 ]] Hamid Istanbouli – a fisherman
by profession, who was also a traditional storyteller (//hakawati//) and thus
interested in cinema – projected films on the wall of a Turkish
hammam.[[ ^97 ]] In 1939 the Roxy opened, followed in 1942 by the
"Empire":[[ ^98 ]]

<blockquote
  By the mid-1950s there were four cinemas in Tyre, and four more soon opened in
  nearby [[ Nabatieh ]]. Many also hosted live performances by famous actors and
  musicians, serving as community spaces where people from different backgrounds
  came together.[[ ^96 ]]

In 1959, the "Cinema Rivoli of Tyre" opened and quickly became one of the
prime movie theatres of the country. According to
[[ UNIFIL | united-nations-interim-force-in-lebanon ]], it was visited "by
celebrity who's whos of the time, including [[ Jean Marais ]],
[[ Brigitte Bardot ]], [[ Rushdi Abaza | rushdy-abaza ]] and Omar
Hariri."[[ ^99 ]] In 1964, the "Dunia" opened,[[ ^100 ]] two years later
followed by the "Al Hamra Cinema",[[ ^98 ]] which became a venue for some of
the Arab world's most famous performers, like [[ Mahmoud Darwish ]],
[[ Sheikh Imam ]], [[ Ahmed Fouad Negm ]], [[ Wadih el-Safi ]], and
[[ Marcel Khalife ]].[[ ^96 ]]

Meanwhile, two Tyrian artists had a major impact on the development of
Lebanese music: [[ Halim el-Roumi ]] (1919–1983) and Ghazi Kahwaji
(1945–2017). Some sources claim that the famous musician, composer, singer and
actor el-Roumi was born in Tyre to Lebanese parents. However, others suggest
that he was born in [[ Nazareth ]] and moved to Tyre from Palestine.[[ ^101 ]]
For some time, he worked as a teacher at the Jafariya High School there. In
1950 he became director of [[ Radio Lebanon ]]'s music department,[[ ^102 ]]
where he discovered the singer [[ Fairuz ]] and introduced her to the
[[ Rahbani brothers ]].[[ ^103 ]] Roumi composed music for and with them in
close collaborations.[[ ^104 ]]

Kahwaji was Lebanon's first [[ scenographer ]] and for three decades the
artistic general director for the [[ Rahbani brothers ]] and [[ Fairuz ]]. He
used this prominent position to promote "against
[[ confessionalism | confessionalism-religion ]] and
[[ fundamentalism ]]".[[ ^105 ]] Kahwaji, who was also a professor at the
[[ Lebanese University ]] (LU) and the [[ Saint Joseph University ]] in
Beirut,[[ ^106 ]] published between 2008 and 2010 the
[[ sarcastic | sarcasm ]] three-volume book series "Kahwajiyat" about
[[ social injustice | social-justice ]] in the Arab world.[[ ^105 ]]

By then, cultural life in Tyre had been severely affected by armed conflict as
well. In 1975, the commercial "Festivals de Tyr" – organised by Maha al-Khalil
Chalabi, the daughter of feudal landlord and politician Kazem al-Khalil – were
supposed to debut but stopped at the outbreak of the Civil War.[[ ^107 ]]

Some cinemas were damaged by Israeli bombardment in 1982 and all of them
eventually closed down, the last ones in 1989:[[ ^96 ]] the Hamra and the
AK2000.[[ ^98 ]]

In the mid-nineties though, first the idea of a commercial Tyre International
Festival was revived. It has been organised since then annually in the ancient
site of the Roman hippodrome, featuring international artists like
[[ Elton John ]] and [[ Sarah Brightman ]],[[ ^108 ]] as well as Lebanese
stars [[ Wadih El Safi ]], [[ Demis Roussos ]], [[ Kadim Al-Saher ]],
[[ Melhem Barakat ]], [[ Julia Boutros ]], and [[ Majida El Roumi ]],[[ ^50 ]]
the daughter of Halim el-Roumi.

In 2006, the "Centre de Lecture et d'Animation Culturelle" (C.L.A.C.) was
opened by Tyre's municipality as the first public library of the city, with
support from the Lebanese Ministry of Culture and the French Embassy in
Beirut. It is located in the historical building of the "Beit Daoud" next to
the "Beit El Medina", the former Mamluk House, in the old town.[[ ^109 ]]

In 2014, the NGO Tiro Association for Arts rehabilitated the defunct cinema Al
Hamra under the leadership of "Palestinian-Lebanese street theater performer,
actor, comedian, and theater director"[[ ^110 ]]
[[ Kassem Istanbouli | https://ar.wikipedia.org/wiki/مستخدم:Kassem%20istanbouli ]]
(*1986). His grandfather was one of the founders of cinema in Tyre and his
father used to repair cinema projectors.[[ ^97 ]]

In 2018, the Istanbouli Theatre troupe rehabilitated and moved to the Rivoli
Cinema,[[ ^111 ]] which had been closed since 1988,[[ ^112 ]] to establish the
non-commercial Lebanese National Theater as a free cultural space with free
entrance and a special focus on training children and youth in arts. It also
runs the "Mobile Peace Bus", which is decorated with [[ graffiti ]] of
Lebanese cultural icons, to promote arts in the villages of the neighbouring
countryside.[[ ^113 ]] Istanbouli has argued:

<blockquote
  In Tyre, we have 400 shops for [[ shisha | hookah ]], one library, and one
  theatre. But if there are places, people will come.[[ ^114 ]]

In 2019, the film //Manara// (Arabic for "lighthouse") by Lebanese director
[[ Zayn Alexander ]], who shot the movie at the Al Fanar resort in Tyre, won
the Laguna Sud Award for Best Short Film at the [[ Venice Days ]] Strand
festival.[[ ^115 ]]

<ul
  <li
    <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/RoxyCinemaRuins_TyreSour_RomanDeckert16082019.jpg/250px-RoxyCinemaRuins_TyreSour_RomanDeckert16082019.jpg" alt="The ruins of the building that used to house the Empire cinema, 2019" height="149" width="200"

    The ruins of the building that used to house the Empire cinema, 2019

  <li
